---
import Read from '../layouts/Read.astro';
import { questions, circles } from '../lib/atlas.js';
---
<Read title="Community — find who thinks like you, and who doesn't — Svapna" description={`Answer ${questions.length} old questions, see which thinkers stand nearest and furthest from you, and find your circle. What is open now, and what is being built.`} path="/community" crumbs={[{ name: 'Community', path: '/community' }]}>
  <p class="small crumb"><a href="/">Svapna</a> · Community</p>
  <h1>Answers, <em>compared.</em></h1>
  <p class="lede">The atlas records where thinkers across four thousand years stand on fourteen questions. A visitor can add their own answers and see who stands nearest and who stands furthest.</p>
  <p><a class="go big" href="/you">Answer the questions →</a></p>

  <section>
    <h2>Open now</h2>
    <div class="steps">
      <div><b>Your answers</b><div><p>{questions.length} questions, one tap each. They stay in your browser.</p><a class="go" href="/you">Start →</a></div></div>
      <div><b>Nearest and furthest</b><div><p>After three answers, the thinkers who stand closest to you and the ones who stand opposite, each with the passage that puts them there.</p><a class="go" href="/thinkers">All the thinkers →</a></div></div>
      <div><b>Circles</b><div><p>{circles.length} loose gatherings of thinkers who lean the same way. Your answers point to the ones you lean toward.</p><a class="go" href="/circles">See the circles →</a></div></div>
      <div><b>Stickers</b><div><p>Small marks for the way you lean and for what you have read. They gather on your page as you go.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Being built</h2>
    <p class="def">None of this is open yet. It arrives in this order, and nothing is switched on without a plain account of what others can see.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div><b>Signing in</b><div><p>An optional account, so your answers follow you between devices.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>The world today</b><div><p>A map of how people answer now, by country, set beside four thousand years of answers.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>Members in circles</b><div><p>The circles open to people as well as thinkers: a way to find those who lean as you do, and those who don't.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>Debate</b><div><p>Two members, one question, opposite answers, each arguing from the passages.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>Checking the atlas</b><div><p>Members say where a passage really belongs on its scale, and why. The readings are drafts; this is how they get better.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How people are treated here</h2>
    <p class="def">Disagreement is the point; contempt is not. No recruiting, no advice nobody asked for. <a href="/about/conduct">The code of conduct</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Read>
